<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Jailolo Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1250 CE – 1551 CE (approx.) · capital Jailolo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the four kingdoms of the North Moluccas, on the island of Halmahera, absorbed by its rival Ternate in 1551.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Jailolo on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halmahera">Halmahera</a> was one of the four Moluccan kingdoms, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ternate-sultanate">Ternate</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tidore-sultanate">Tidore</a> and Bacan, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moloku-kie-raha">Moloku Kie Raha</a>. Local tradition held it to be the oldest of the four. Its rulers accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in the late 15th century, as Muslim traders brought the clove trade under their influence.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Jailolo controlled parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halmahera">Halmahera</a>, a source of sago and food for the clove-growing islands, and took part in the clove trade with Javanese and Malay merchants. Its sultans were often in conflict with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ternate-sultanate">Ternate</a> and sometimes looked to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tidore-sultanate">Tidore</a> and the Spanish for support.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ternate-sultanate">Ternate</a>, backed by the Portuguese, attacked Jailolo in the 1530s and 1550s, and in 1551 its sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katarabumi">Katarabumi</a> was forced to submit. Jailolo became part of the Ternate Sultanate. Its title was revived briefly in the 19th century by rebels against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jailolo-sultanate.jpg" alt="Jailolo Sultanate" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: HHEHUM · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jailolo_Dutch_East_Indies.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1250 CE – 1551 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jailolo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Katarabumi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>North Moluccan Malay, Galela and other North Halmahera languages</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jailolo, Gilolo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1400&amp;focus=jailolo-sultanate" aria-label="Jailolo Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8219 2137 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jailolo Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8539 2393L8538 2404"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8538 2404h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8539 2393h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8539 2393h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jailolo. <a href="/atlas?year=1400&amp;focus=jailolo-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

